foreign  [Music]  we will share some best practices on how  to keep your Google kubernetes engine or  gke clusters up to date and how to  choose an upgrade strategy that is best  suited for your needs and maximize the  availability and reliability of your gke  environments first we recommend that you  have multiple gke environments to  minimize risk and unwanted downtime by  allowing you to test upgrades on a  staging environment before upgrading the  production environment  the production environment is mainly  used for serving live traffic to end  users and Mission critical business  applications  staging environment is where you test  changes from the new gke version to make  sure that everything is working as  expected before updating the production  environment  test environment is used to run  performance benchmarks test and QA  workloads against the same version which  you want to run in production  development environment is where you  carry your active deployment with the  same version that is running in your  production environment  next be aware that kubernetes releases  updates on a regular basis  those updates include bug fixes security  patches and introducing new features  gke allows you to incorporate kubernetes  updates into your clusters automatically  using gke release channels and the auto  upgrade feature  gka release Channel offers you the  ability to balance between stability and  a feature set of the version deployed in  a cluster  it is recommended to enroll your cluster  in an appropriate release channel to  allow Google to upgrade your cluster and  its notebooks automatically  however you can control the timing and  scope of the upgrades using maintenance  windows and exclusions that fit your  business needs  to keep your clusters up to date with  the latest gke version and kubernetes  updates Google recommends to use the  stable or regular channel to prioritize  the stability of your production  workloads and use the same channel as  production in staging environment to  ensure your tests are indicative of the  version your production will be upgraded  to  keep in mind that Google publishes the  information about new updates on the gke  release notes page check the section for  your clusters release channel to get  detailed information about the latest  update  each release Channel offers a default  gke version which is used for auto  upgrades  new patch releases become available at  least one week before turning into the  default version and new minor releases  will become available at least four  weeks before turning into the default  option  once a new version is available we  recommend that you test it in a staging  environments and upgrade your production  environments before that version becomes  the default one  this approach will give you more time  for testing and allow more control over  the production environment  gke will skip Auto upgrade if your  clusters were already upgraded manually  as mentioned earlier you can create a  maintenance window that will ensure that  the gke upgrades are performed during  off-peak business hours  let me walk you through step by step how  you can set up a maintenance window  first log into your Google Cloud console  then hand over to kubernetes engine and  select clusters  select the desired cluster and scroll  down to automation  under this section enable the  maintenance window by simply checking  the Box  here you can either select the weekly  editor which allows you to select the  days of the week on which the  maintenance will occur  or you can select the custom editor  option which allows you to pick the  start and end times for your maintenance  in our example we will choose the weekly  editor and set Sunday as the day when  the maintenance will occur click on Save  changes  excellent the maintenance window has  been created successfully  finally let's discuss a couple of  different strategies you can use with  your gke clusters  search upgrades in this strategy the  nodes are upgraded in a rolling window  you can control how many nodes can be  upgraded at once and how disruptive  upgrades are to the workloads  blue green upgrades this strategy will  keep your existing notes available for  rollback while the workloads are  validated on the new node configuration  for more information about each of these  strategies check out these documents  and to get more information on best  practices for upgrading gke clusters  refer to this document thank you for  watching

